{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a75a4ebabaa4d13","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013 (FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack)\n11\t\n12\tFull decomposition (not triaged): the strategy spans two components (KServe,\n13\tPlatform Operator), multiple teams, and multiple work streams (image\n14\tremediation, CI enforcement, compliance evidence assembly and submission,\n15\tdocumentation), so neither the below-threshold nor the docs-only triage path\n16\tapplies.\n17\t\n18\t## Epic List\n19\t\n20\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n21\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | FIPS crypto usage audit and remediation-path investigation for serving-stack images | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | FIPS 140-3 validated-crypto remediation for KServe serving-stack images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | FIPS 140-3 validated-crypto remediation for the Platform Operator image | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package assembly and submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 | FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n27\t\n28\tPriorities: E001–E004 are P0 because they enable or deliver the P0 HLRs\n29\t(HLR-1, HLR-2); an epic blocking all P0 remediation work (the audit) is\n30\timplicitly P0. E005 is P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception — the\n31\tstrategy frontmatter priority is Critical (Critical→P0); it is exempt from\n32\tpriority inheritance and does not push priority upstream.\n33\t\n34\t## Dependency DAG\n35\t\n36\t```mermaid\n37\tgraph TD\n38\t    E001[\"E001 FIPS audit (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 KServe image remediation\"]\n39\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 Platform Operator image remediation\"]\n40\t    E002 --> E004[\"E004 Evidence package + submission\"]\n41\t    E003 --> E004\n42\t    E002 --> E005[\"E005 FIPS documentation\"]\n43\t    E003 --> E005\n44\t    E004 --> E005\n45\t```\n46\t\n47\t## DAG Justification\n48\t\n49\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n50\t|------|------|-----------|\n51\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The audit determines which KServe images link non-validated crypto and the remediation path per finding — the answer changes what the remediation epic does. True gate: `rewrite` (remediation scope/approach changes with findings; the epic exists either way, so no branch decomposition). |\n52\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Same gate for the Platform Operator image; both remediation epics run in parallel once unblocked (different components/repos, Rule 14). |\n53\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 13 (artifact-producing → consuming Implementation) | The evidence package assembles the module inventory updates, build attestations, and FIPS-cluster test results that the KServe remediation produces; it cannot be assembled before remediation completes. |\n54\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 13 (artifact-producing → consuming Implementation) | Same: evidence for the Platform Operator image depends on its remediation completing. |\n55\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation states the posture that remediation establishes. |\n56\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n57\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same — docs describe what was built and submitted. |\n58\t\n59\tE001 is an Investigation, not an Implementation epic, so it is not a Rule 11\n60\tparty for the docs epic; E005 is transitively blocked by it through E002/E003.\n61\t\n62\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n63\t\n64\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n65\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n66\t| HLR-1 — serving-stack images run on FIPS-enabled clusters with validated crypto | P0 | E002, E003 | Split per component/team (Rule 1): KServe images (E002) and the Platform Operator image (E003). |\n67\t| HLR-2 — compliance evidence package assembled and submitted through review board to certification lab | P0 | E001 (module inventory input), E004 (assembly and submission) | The audit produces the module inventory; E004 assembles the full package and submits it. |\n68\t| HLR-3 — CI enforces FIPS regressions via a gating check | P1 | E002, E003 (bundled) | Bundled per surface: the gating FIPS check is divided into each component's remediation epic (Scope + AC), because it guards the exact build surfaces the P0 remediation converts and shares the same detection tooling and build pipeline — it is not separately schedulable, and a standalone enforcement epic would be a work-type split (check 6 shape). |\n69\t| HLR-4 — documentation states FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | P2 | E005 | docs-authoring epic; content depends on remediation outcomes (Rule 11, always last). |\n70\t\n71\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n72\t\n73\t## Health Warnings\n74\t\n75\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (health warning, decomposition proceeded with\n76\t   available information): the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n77\t   Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no NFRs, and no\n78\t   technical approach section. Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the\n79\t   HLRs. Team assignments are inferred: KServe → RHOAI Serving (per\n80\t   architecture context `architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`); Platform Operator\n81\t   → RHOAI Platform (assumed — no context file states its owner); the\n82\t   documentation epic is assigned to RHOAI Serving as the serving-stack owner.\n83\t   Verify team assignments before scheduling.\n84\t2. **Platform Operator architecture-context gap**: Platform Operator has no\n85\t   file under `.context/architecture-context/`, so it is not corroborated as\n86\t   \"in the platform\" by the context set. The strategy's premise treats it as\n87\t   an existing shipping component whose images need remediation (not\n88\t   provisioning), so no repo-onboarding/konflux-onboarding chain was\n89\t   generated. `architecture_claims` is scored 0 for E003 accordingly. If\n90\t   Platform Operator work is actually greenfield, re-decompose with the\n91\t   Rule 20 onboarding chain.\n92\t3. **\"Serving data plane\" scope ambiguity (scope trap)**: the problem\n93\t   statement says \"KServe components and the serving data plane link\n94\t   non-validated crypto libraries\", while the Affected Components list names\n95\t   only KServe and Platform Operator. This decomposition interprets the\n96\t   serving data plane as the KServe-managed serving images plus the Platform\n97\t   Operator image. Architecture context shows KServe depends on Serving\n98\t   Runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit) for model execution — if those runtime\n99\t   images are meant, an additional remediation epic for the Serving Runtimes\n100\t   component is needed. Confirm with the strategy author.\n101\t4. **External schedule gates are risks, not ACs**: certification-lab\n102\t   turnaround (historically six to nine months) and security review board\n103\t   cadence are externally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering\n104\t   effort. They are recorded as Risks/Notes on the evidence-package epic;\n105\t   its acceptance criteria end at the engineering completion point we\n106\t   control (package assembled, internally validated, submitted through the\n107\t   review board, handed off to the lab).\n108\t5. **HLR-3 (P1) bundled into P0 remediation epics**: recorded in the HLR\n109\t   Traceability Matrix above — the gating CI check shares build surfaces and\n110\t   detection tooling with the P0 remediation and is not independently\n111\t   schedulable.\n112\t6. **Docs epic priority**: E005 is P0 by the docs-authoring priority\n113\t   exception (strategy priority Critical → P0), not by inheritance; this is\n114\t   per rule, not a priority inversion.\n115\t\n116\t## Tiered Delivery\n117\t\n118\tThe external dependency (accredited certification lab, security review board)\n119\thas uncertain timing, and the strategy delivers partial value without it:\n120\t\n121\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: E001 → E002 ∥ E003, plus the assembly work\n122\t  in E004 — the serving stack runs correctly on FIPS-enabled OpenShift\n123\t  clusters with validated crypto, and CI enforces FIPS regressions. This is\n124\t  deliverable regardless of certification timing.\n125\t- **Tier 2 (externally gated)**: the certification milestone — evidence\n126\t  submission processing, lab evaluation, and review-board sign-off. These are\n127\t  gated by external parties, not by any epic this strategy produces, so no\n128\t  separate Tier 2 epic is generated (Rule: third-party-resolved dependencies\n129\t  are modeled as preconditions with tiered delivery, not epics). HLR-2's\n130\t  completion point is submission; the certification outcome is tracked as a\n131\t  risk on the evidence-package epic.\n132\t\n133\t## Component Mapping Notes\n134\t\n135\t- E001's audit spans both affected components (KServe images and the Platform\n136\t  Operator image); it is assigned component `KServe` as the most-relevant\n137\t  canonical name (the serving stack is its primary scope) — span noted here.\n138\t- E004's evidence package covers the whole serving stack; assigned component\n139\t  `KServe` as the most-relevant canonical name — span noted here.\n140\t- E005 uses canonical component `Documentation` (docs content work).\n141\t- All component values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n142\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-899ae1005764a1dd","agent_id":"a138c43316943d2b2","session_id":"fe0d12c6-319a-