{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8cbd0833f09e9251","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9013-E002\n3\ttitle: FIPS 140-3 validated-crypto remediation for KServe serving-stack images\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n5\tcomponent: KServe\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-9013-E001\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9013-E001\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: If the audit finds no non-validated crypto in KServe images,\n15\t    or requires upstream dependency releases that are not yet available, the remediation\n16\t    scope and approach for the affected images are rewritten to match the findings;\n17\t    the epic is not removed since remediation and the gating build check proceed on\n18\t    whatever findings exist.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 0\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: -1\n25\t  external_dependency: 0\n26\t  human_process_gates: -1\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: Medium\n32\tai_implementability_score: 1\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\t---\n36\t# FIPS 140-3 validated-crypto remediation for KServe serving-stack images\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tThis epic makes every KServe serving-stack image run correctly on FIPS-enabled\n41\tOpenShift clusters using validated cryptographic modules, with no non-validated\n42\tcrypto in the serving request path, and wires a gating FIPS regression check\n43\tinto the KServe build pipeline so any build that reintroduces non-validated\n44\tcrypto usage fails. It applies the per-image remediation plan produced by the\n45\tFIPS crypto audit to the KServe component images.\n46\t\n47\t## Scope\n48\t\n49\t- Apply the audit's remediation plan to all KServe component images: swap\n50\t  non-validated crypto libraries and base images for validated ones, upgrade\n51\t  or rebuild dependencies, and adjust build configuration as the plan\n52\t  specifies, removing non-validated crypto from the request path.\n53\t- Update the module inventory entries for every remediated KServe image so\n54\t  the compliance evidence package reflects the post-remediation state.\n55\t- Add a gating FIPS regression check to the KServe build pipeline that fails\n56\t  any KServe build reintroducing non-validated crypto usage (this epic owns\n57\t  the enforcement for the KServe image surfaces it converts).\n58\t- Validate the remediated images on a FIPS-enabled OpenShift cluster:\n59\t  InferenceServices deploy and serve requests correctly in both serverless\n60\t  and raw deployment modes, with the request path using validated crypto.\n61\t\n62\t## Acceptance Criteria\n63\t\n64\t- Every KServe serving-stack image contains no non-validated crypto in the\n65\t  request path, verified by re-running the audit's scanning method on the\n66\t  remediated images.\n67\t- Remediated KServe images deploy InferenceServices and serve inference\n68\t  requests correctly on a FIPS-enabled OpenShift cluster, in both serverless\n69\t  and raw deployment modes.\n70\t- The KServe build pipeline carries a gating FIPS check that fails a build\n71\t  which reintroduces non-validated crypto usage and passes the remediated\n72\t  builds (build pipeline green with the check in place).\n73\t- The module inventory is updated to reflect the post-remediation state of\n74\t  every KServe image.\n75\t\n76\t## HLR Traceability\n77\t\n78\t- **HLR-1 (P0)** — primary: all serving-stack images run correctly on\n79\t  FIPS-enabled clusters using validated crypto modules (KServe images).\n80\t- **HLR-3 (P1)** — bundled: the gating FIPS regression check for KServe\n81\t  builds is carried in this epic rather than a standalone enforcement epic\n82\t  because it guards the exact build surfaces this epic converts and shares\n83\t  its detection tooling and pipeline wiring — it is not separately\n84\t  schedulable from the remediation of the same surfaces.\n85\t\n86\t## Risks / Notes\n87\t\n88\t- Remediation actions depend on the FIPS crypto audit's findings; if a\n89\t  finding requires an upstream dependency release that is not yet available,\n90\t  the remediation approach for that image is rewritten accordingly.\n91\t- Security review board sign-off at defined milestones is a mandatory human\n92\t  gate on the compliance program.\n93\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-899ae1005764a1dd","agent_id":"a138c43316943d2b2","session_id":"fe0d12c6-319a-4740-b115-3713d369a771","uuid":"813f7311-4ff5-48ef-9fd9-fdc5dc1d943a","timestamp":"2026-10-26T16:17:34.846Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8cbd0833f09e9251","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
